; e188_apply_spine.prog -- the E188 fixture: an `$apply` arm for a
; defunctionalization site whose `def-ctx` FAILS.
;
; `arm-body` (lib/lowering/upper/closconv.chiral) has two arms for a `cs-g`
; site.  The second one, for a global whose `def-ctx` answers `none`, read
; `(c-lit-i 0)` under a comment saying it was unreachable.  It is reached, and
; the literal was the WHOLE BODY of an `$apply` dispatcher arm.  E188 replaces
; it with the call spine `(g cap0..cap_{k-1} a0..a_{d-1})`.
;
; ⚑ THE `def-ctx` FAILURE HERE IS THE ANNOTATION, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.
; `def-ctx` peels with `peel-lam-exact`, which demands a PLAIN lam chain and
; skips no annotation (`closconv.chiral`, its own comment).  `compile-fn` peels
; with `strip-lams`, which calls `strip-ann` first (`lower.chiral:215-218`).  So
; `(the T (lam (a x y) ..))` fails `def-ctx` and lowers perfectly, which is the
; one shape where the spine's value is OBSERVABLE.
;
; ⚑ EN-20's OWN `box-f` SHAPE CANNOT CARRY THIS ROW, MEASURED 2026-09-04.  A
; global whose BODY is genuinely shallower than its TYPE does not lower either:
; `rewrite-one` (`closconv-driver.chiral:251-253`) leaves a def whose `def-ctx`
; fails untouched, so `compile-fn` refuses it on the same `strip-lams` arm.
; Under E188 the spine then calls it and the compile is REFUSED by name --
; `compile-main <- use <- $apply1 <- box-f: extern does not lower: body is not
; a lambda chain` -- instead of silently answering `0`.  That is the element's
; whole argument (SPEC §3.3, three answers collapsing to one) and it is a
; strictly better outcome, but it is a refusal and not a value, so a fixture
; built on it would grade R1 to R3 `absent`.  The SPEC's §1 sentence that
; EN-20's fixture "prints 30 on both lines" is corrected by that measurement.
;
; ⚑ THE THREE SUPPLIED ARGUMENTS ARE DISTINCT AND THE BODY IS NON-COMMUTATIVE.
; §5's M4 permutes which binder one spine argument reads while keeping the
; argument count.  A permutation of equal arguments, or of arguments under `+`
; alone, prints `direct`'s own answer and reddens nothing.  `(+ a (- x y))`
; over 10, 35, 15 costs one operator and makes the permutation visible.
;
; HOME: tools/test/samples/, not prog/.  Phase 7 discovers roots with
; `grep -rl '^(def compile-main' lib prog` (tools/test/run-tests.sh:175); this
; file needs a `compile-main` for the value rows, so under prog/ it would be a
; second new root and move the root census twice.
;
; It ASSERTS NOTHING and always exits 0.  Every comparison lives in bash, in
; tools/test/apply-spine.sh.  A fixture that grades itself can be wrong twice in
; the same direction.
;
;   direct: 30    the same global called directly           -> R1
;   apply : 30    the same global through the dispatcher    -> R2, the deliverable
;   sib   : -10   the honestly-shaped sibling, `some` path  -> R3
(import "prelude/prelude")
(import "ports/stdio")

; def-ctx FAILS (the annotation), compile-fn SUCCEEDS (strip-ann).  This is the
; global whose $apply arm was the literal 0.
(def e188-boxf (-> I64 I64 I64 I64)
  (the (-> I64 I64 I64 I64) (lam (a x y) (+ a (- x y)))))

; the honestly-shaped sibling: three lams for three domains, so def-ctx answers
; `some` and arm-body takes the UNCHANGED path.  Same family key.
(def e188-sib (-> I64 I64 I64 I64)
  (lam (a x y) (- a (- x y))))

; a saturated higher-order call: this is what becomes the `$apply<i>` call.
(def e188-use (-> (-> I64 I64 I64 I64) I64 I64 I64 I64)
  (lam (f a x y) (f a x y)))

(def compile-main (=> I64 I64)
  (lam (n)
    (let ((_ (put (str-cat "direct: " (str-cat (i64->str (e188-boxf 10 35 15)) "\n")))))
    (let ((_ (put (str-cat "apply : " (str-cat (i64->str (e188-use e188-boxf 10 35 15)) "\n")))))
    (let ((_ (put (str-cat "sib   : " (str-cat (i64->str (e188-use e188-sib 10 35 15)) "\n")))))
      0)))))
